# Keel: nightly backup of every database and every uploads directory.
#
# 03:30 local: after the day's orders have stopped and before the aggregation
# the console reads in the morning. A backup taken mid-service competes for the
# same disk the kitchen is writing orders to.
#
# Output goes to a log rather than to root's mail, which nobody on this box
# reads. Whether it actually ran is answered in the console (System → backups),
# from the manifest on disk — not from the fact that this file exists.
#
# ⚠️ Install this as a **root-owned copy**, never as a symlink into the repo:
#
#     install -m 0644 -o root -g root deploy/keel-backup.cron /etc/cron.d/keel-backup
#
# cron refuses any file in /etc/cron.d that is not owned by root or is
# group-writable, and the checkout here is owned by deploy-keel at 664. It
# refuses it *silently* — the complaint goes to cron's own journal
# ("WRONG FILE OWNER") and nowhere else, so the file sits in /etc/cron.d
# looking installed while no copy is taken. That is exactly how this box went
# three nights without a backup after 8 Aug 2026. Editing this file therefore
# means re-installing it; git pull does not update /etc/cron.d.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

30 3 * * * root /usr/local/bin/keel-backup >> /var/log/keel-backup.log 2>&1
